  "summary": "NPR/KQED report argues that while Flock ALPRs have drawn public backlash, privacy experts view AI-powered real-time crime center software like Axon's Fusus as a broader surveillance concern because it aggregates ALPR, body cams, drones, 911, gunshot detection and private surveillance feeds into a single pane of glass. Fusus adoption has more than doubled since Axon's 2024 acquisition, and experts call for legislative guardrails like retention limits and audits.",
  "key_quotes": [
    {
      "quote": "real-time crime centers have not just ALPR cameras and sensors, but video analytics and drones and essentially a way to create a visual single-pane-of-glass vision of what's going on in a city, with time machine capabilities where you can go back and investigate crime.",
      "paragraph_idx": 16
    },
    {
      "quote": "The number of law enforcement agencies using the tool has more than doubled since then, according to Axon.",
      "paragraph_idx": 20
    },
    {
      "quote": "What happens if there is a government that wants to silence a particular group or even a particular person? And you just built the architecture of surveillance that makes that super-easy",
      "paragraph_idx": 30
    },
    {
      "quote": "lawmakers could act by limiting how long data is stored, for instance, mandating regular audits or restricting surveillance near sensitive sites like abortion clinics or immigration courts.",
      "paragraph_idx": 31
    }
  ],
